    description: "FIXED 2026-09-15, reported with a screenshot: \"mira la cantidad de cosas que no se ven en el modo light\", then \"un montón, revísalos todos\". He was right about the scale: 41 of the 43 filtered images in the app were affected. TWO CAUSES. (1) THE UNIT-CARD HEADER keeps its dark faction gradient in light mode - that colour IS the faction's identity - but every piece of text on it is a palette class, so the ink inverted to near-black on dark red. It is now a FIXED-DARK surface whose ink is pinned to the values measured from the dark theme, so it renders identically in both. (2) ICONS DRAWN WITH AN INLINE `filter`. The light theme works by reassigning palette variables, so classes follow it and inline filters do not: `brightness(0) invert(1)` paints white whatever the theme, and in light mode that is white on a 0.87-0.99 panel. The two constants `STAT_ICON_FILTER` and `TYPE_ICON_FILTER` now resolve `var(--icon-tint-gold)` / `var(--icon-tint)`, which fixed roughly thirty icons at once; the slot panel and the Mark buttons followed. The two icons on genuinely dark surfaces keep `--icon-tint-fixed` and stay white - tinting those by theme is what hid 18 of 21 symbols when light mode first shipped. AND A THIRD, FOUND BY THE SWEEP: an OPACITY-MODIFIED text class never received its light-mode override. `.text-amber-700` has one; `text-amber-700/80` compiles to a different class that color-mixes the MIRRORED variable, so the \"PROFILE\" heading stayed a washed-out light gold at 1.25:1 against its own panel. Five such variants now have matching rules. The border overrides had already needed exactly this and the text ones were missed. THE SWEEP ITSELF IS THE LESSON, and it went wrong THREE TIMES before it was right: first it compared oklch LIGHTNESS against WCAG LUMINANCE and reported 120 phantom failures; then its fallback background read `document.body`, which is transparent, so light-on-light looked like dark-on-black; then its canvas composited alpha colours over black and made them look far darker than they paint. Every one of those produced confident, specific, WRONG findings. A contrast checker must be calibrated against a known pair before it is believed - a mid grey on white is about 6.5:1, and any tool that says otherwise is the thing that is broken."
