    id: "ki-light-mode-01",
    status: "fixed",
    title: "Light mode, done by inverting the palette rather than restyling the app",
    description: "ADDED 2026-09-13 (atypicalhero: \"I, too, would like a light mode\"). The app has roughly 2900 colour utilities across its components ' 1756 zinc alone ' so restyling them with `dark:` variants was never the move. It runs TAILWIND V4, which compiles every palette colour to `var(--color-<ramp>-<step>)`, so reassigning those variables under `[data-theme=\"light\"]` flips the entire app without a single component being touched. THE RULE IS A MIRROR, NOT A HAND-PICKED PALETTE: step 100 takes 900's value, 200 takes 800's, and so on. That is what makes it coherent rather than merely inverted ' the app is written \"light ink on a dark panel\" everywhere, and mirroring turns every one of those into \"dark ink on a light panel\" while preserving the exact contrast the design already had. Accents come along for free: amber-400, the brand colour on every card, becomes amber-600, which is the tone that actually reads on white. White and black are swapped for the same reason, which also turns the modal scrims from black to white. `scripts/gen_light_theme.cjs` generates the block from the BUILT css ' Tailwind's real values, not remembered ones ' and where Tailwind never emitted a mirror partner (the step 50s, which a dark app has no use for) the generator was fed them by a temporary `@source inline` probe rather than having values typed in from memory. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER, not only by build: the landing page, the announcement banner and the army builder all render correctly, and the one control that looked washed out at screenshot scale measured as 44% lightness text on a 92% ground, which is fine. KNOWN LIMIT: the campaign Cogitator screens use hand-written CRT greens (`#020702` and friends) rather than palette tokens and stay dark on purpose ' that is a deliberate terminal look, not an oversight. Stored per device in `c40k_theme`, stamped on <html> before React mounts so there is no flash of the wrong theme. TUNED 2026-09-13 after Rigzar looked at it: \"muchas letras y logos se pierden ' todos los simbolos se pierden porque son blancos ' el cuadro de anuncios se ve raro ' las letras doradas cuesta leerlas\". All four were the same two mistakes. (1) THE PURE MIRROR PRESERVES THE LIGHTNESS DISTANCE between text and its panel, which sounds correct and is not: dark ink on a light ground needs MORE separation than light ink on a dark one at the same distance, so every muted step read as broken rather than subtle ' `text-zinc-700` landed at 87% lightness on a 99% page and `text-amber-800` at 92%, i.e. invisible. Fixed by measuring which steps the app uses as TEXT versus as PANELS (zinc-400: 164 text / 0 backgrounds; zinc-800: 0 text / 140 backgrounds) and moving only the text ones as variables, pulling them into a 38-56% band; the structural steps keep the mirror, and the handful of places a structural step is used as text are overridden one utility at a time. Gold now sits at 44-48% and reads. Panel borders and the gold frames got the same treatment, including the /NN opacity variants, since Tailwind compiles each into its own class and a rule on the bare name never reaches them ' that is why the Preferences window, framed in `border-2 border-amber-800`, had no visible frame at all. (2) FOUR COMPONENTS FORCED SINGLE-COLOUR ART TO WHITE with `filter: brightness(0) invert(1)` inline, so every faction symbol, slot icon and phase icon vanished on a white page. The filter moved into `.symbol-tint` / `.symbol-tint-gold` in index.css where the theme can reach it; `brightness(0)` alone renders the same art black. Verified in the browser afterwards rather than assumed: zero images remain with an `invert` filter, every sampled text class lands in the legible band, and the card border is a visible hairline. ONE TRAP WORTH REMEMBERING: the PWA service worker kept serving the previous build, so the first two rounds of checking measured stale CSS and looked like the fix had not applied ' unregister it and clear caches before believing a styling check. SECOND ROUND, same day: \"pusiste negros los logos de faccion y ahora no se ven a la hora de seleccionar que faccion quieres, al igual que sus letras\". Both were mine. (a) TINTING EVERY SYMBOL BY THEME WAS TOO BLUNT: 18 of the 21 on the faction screen sit on a coloured ROUNDEL whose background is a faction identity colour and stays dark in light mode, so turning them black hid them. Split into `.symbol-tint` (follows the theme, for art on the page ground) and `.symbol-tint-fixed` (always white, for art on its own dark plate); the other three call sites were checked and all sit on theme-following panels, so they keep the first. (b) THE CATEGORY LABELS ' Chaos, Imperium, Xenos ' are inline identity colours (`pillFg`, pale tints made for a near-black page) and measured 2.0-2.8:1 on white. They now have a `pillFgLight` sibling in the catalog, same hue with enough depth; it is a separate value rather than a derivation because nothing about the pale tint implies the dark one. AND THE GOLD AGAIN, which was NOT a contrast problem the second time: the section headings measured about 9:1 on white and still read faint, because they are 11px Cinzel at weight 400 with wide letter-spacing. Light type on a dark ground blooms and looks heavier than it is, so a face tuned in the dark theme comes out anaemic when the polarity flips. Fixed with STROKE rather than more darkness: `.font-cinzel` goes to weight 600 in light mode (already loaded, so no extra fetch) and `.tracking-widest` tightens slightly. A scripted audit over text, borders and button fills now reports zero failures below 3:1 on the landing page, the faction step and the configuration step."
