    description: "FIXED 2026-09-13. Found while checking a report that turned out not to be one: Rigzar thought he could register for an event twice, then corrected himself (\"mentira no me deja, error mio\") ' and he was right, re-registering was never possible, since `register` upserts with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE that keeps the existing row and the button hides once you are in. THE REAL HOLE WAS NEXT TO IT: `assign-list` had NO check of any kind beyond ownership of the roster and an approved registration. A player could therefore change which army they were registered with after registration had closed, after the league was under way, and after playing games ' MY FIRST EXPLANATION OF WHY THAT MATTERED WAS WRONG, and Rigzar's own games list is what disproved it: his two games showed two DIFFERENT armies of his (\"SoH\" on one, \"Hueste de la Palabra Encarnada\" on the other). `event_games` stores its own `reporter_roster_id` and `opponent_roster_id`, captured when the game is reported, and every display joins on THOSE ' so changing your list never rewrote a past game and the recorded history was never at risk. What is actually at stake is a LEAGUE RULE, not data integrity: whether you play the same army all league. That is Dominic's call, not one to derive from the code ' the requirements PDF has no extractable text, so it could not be checked. ANSWERED THE SAME DAY by Dominic ' \"actually a player is allowed to change their army after a loss\" ' so the played-a-game condition is REMOVED. What is left is the deadline, which is what a registration window is for, and it now binds an organiser or admin on their OWN entry too: the referee powers exist to fix other people's problems, not to grant a private exemption. Someone else with the powers can correct them. TWO LOCKS, and the player is told which one applies: once you have any game in the event (reported, confirmed or disputed, either side of it) the list is fixed; and once registration closes, or while the league is closed, nobody changes theirs. Before either, changing it freely is intended and still works ' that is what the registration window is for. The picker disables itself with the reason rather than letting someone choose a list and then be refused on submit, and `get` now resolves the acting-as player as well, so while an admin drives a puppet the page shows the PUPPET's registration and lock instead of the admin's ' every write already went to the puppet, so the two disagreed. and the same rule is enforced server-side, where it actually counts. NOT DELIBERATED FURTHER, and worth a decision later: the organiser cannot override a lock either. That is the strict reading ' if a player genuinely has to change army mid-league, the organiser can currently only delete the games."
