    status: "fixed",
    title: "A unit removed by a codex update became an invisible, free entry that still held its slot",
    description: "FIXED 2026-09-11, found while preparing the September codex updates rather than from a report — Adeptus Custodes 1.01 removes \"Jetbike Custodians\" and the Tyranids update removes the \"Swarmlord\", so this was about to happen to real lists twice. MEASURED FIRST, not assumed: a headless probe built a Necron roster holding a datasheet that does not exist and asked the engine about it. The unit failed to resolve, computeUnitPoints returned 0, and the validator produced ZERO messages about it — the probe printed \"NOTHING. The entry is invisible, costs 0 points, and still holds its slot.\" ROOT CAUSE: a saved army stores each entry by unit NAME, and every consumer in the app handles a name that no longer resolves the same defensive way — resolveUnit() returns undefined, UnitCard does `return null`, and all three points sums (validator total, ArmyList per-slot, ReviewStep) do `: 0`. Individually each is reasonable; together they turn a deleted unit into a free, invisible ghost that still occupies its slot, so the player sees a slot heading reading \"1 unit - 0 pts\" with no card under it and an army that silently got cheaper. FIX, in three parts. New engine/unitRenames.ts holds RENAMED_UNITS (mapped forward on load in both importRoster and the persist migration, so a rename is invisible to the player — the same shape as RENAMED_ARCHETYPES one file over) and REMOVED_UNITS (a note per deleted datasheet). DETECTION DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DEPEND ON THAT TABLE: the validator flags ANY entry that fails to resolve, so a removal nobody recorded is still caught, just described in general terms. ArmyList renders a MissingUnitCard where UnitCard used to render nothing, with the reason and a Remove button. The validator error runs FIRST, before every other check, because until it is resolved every points and slot number below it is describing a different army than the player's. VERIFIED three ways: the same probe now returns exactly one error naming the unit and none on a healthy army; a rename maps forward and the table is faction-scoped (unit names are only unique within a faction — \"Warriors\" is a Necron datasheet AND a Tyranid one); and in the running app a seeded Necron list holding a Swarmlord shows the red card in the HQ slot and puts the error at the top of the Validation panel. NOT YET LISTED IN REMOVED_UNITS: the Custodes and Tyranid removals, because neither codex has been applied — naming them early would report a removal that has not happened.",
